    "work": {
      "description": "Round 4 (Sol 6 r3 B5). PARENT = an active lease held by a human principal on a host. CHILD = a session or job that names that lease_id (its own lease). Rule: at most ONE active parent per (human principal, host); children of that parent never consume a second slot (a session under one's own lease is allowed); a child naming any other lease, or a second parent, is refused 409 account_busy. Atomicity: the authority inserts the parent under a UNIQUE (principal_id, host_id) index for active parents inside the admission transaction; the helper records the parent lease_id per account under an exclusive lock (O_EXCL claim file) and accepts session-open/unit-start only for that lease_id. Both layers key on the same parent lease_id, so under concurrent requests exactly one parent wins in both. Agents are not limited (per-unit isolation). Oracles: admit_parent(), helper_claim(), work_admission(). Round 5: helper session-open and friend unit-start carry --parent-lease; the claim is released only after a proven close of the parent (claim-release) Round 6: a claim is removed ONLY by claim-release with that close proof (race-safe tombstone rename). claim-reconcile during A8 never removes: a claim the authority omits becomes 'orphaned-quarantined' (account blocked, operator alerted) and one naming another parent is a 'conflict'. Round 7 (Sol 6 r6): quarantine has an admission effect: a quarantined account accepts no new parent and no child, including children of the old parent, until an operator claim-clear with the close proof. Round 8 (Sol 6 r7): the helper states the same single rule; claim-clear is the operator's separate root-only program (helper-config x-operator-clear).",
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "required": [
        "max_active_parents_per_human_per_host",
        "children_share_parent_slot"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "max_active_parents_per_human_per_host": {
          "const": 1
        },
        "children_share_parent_slot": {
          "const": true
        }
      }
    }
